    48	
    49	## A1.0 Why amend before starting
    50	
    51	The review found that the pre-registration, as signed, could be *passed by a
    52	proof that does not protect the project*. H0 pre-registered that a
    53	machine-checked proof would exist, but not **what it must prove**. That gap
    54	is precisely the drift-vector formal-methods theater needs: months into the
    55	modeling, the provable-in-practice properties drift toward whatever the
    56	model makes easy, and "the proof holds" quietly becomes "the proof of
    57	whatever we managed to prove holds." The agreement gate, as written, would
    58	judge fidelity only *after* any such drift, by the same parties who drifted.
    59	
    60	This amendment closes that gap by pre-registering the property list
    61	(A1.2), the degraded-policy semantics (A1.2.1), the adversary model (A1.3),
    62	and the tooling rationale and choice discipline (A1.4). It also resolves
    63	one design hole the review surfaced (manifest authority, A1.5), states the
    64	temporal-anchor semantics precisely (A1.6), and hardens the agreement gate
    65	from a confirmation task into a falsification task (A1.7). The original
    66	objectives, bands, and invariants are not weakened; this amendment *adds*
    67	resolution and evidence obligations (A1.8).
    68	
    69	---
    70	
    71	## A1.1 Clarification of the H0 falsifier
    72	
    73	The original's phrasing — "if the guarantee cannot be proven, there is
    74	nothing worth building" — could be misread as one-shot: first failed proof
    75	attempt, project over. That is not the claim and never was. The method is
    76	iterative, as all modeling is: model, attempt proof, find the flaw, revise,
    77	re-prove — with each revision recorded in the journal.
    78	
    79	Iteration within scope is ordinary work. **Falsification is scope
    80	reduction.** Precisely: H0 (as amended) is falsified if any A1.2 property
    81	cannot be discharged without one of the following:
    82	
    83	- **weakening the property** as pre-registered;
    84	- **demoting** a property from `[model]`/`[proof]` to `[assumption]`;
    85	- **excluding** a declared A1.3 adversary capability;
    86	- **changing the construction so materially** that it no longer implements
    87	  the dual-signature + temporal-anchor design of §3.1.
    88	
    89	Any such weakening requires a further signed, dated amendment stating what
    90	was weakened and why; absent that amendment, Phase 1 does not begin. This
    91	gives a future reader a concrete way to see whether Band 0 succeeded,
    92	failed, or changed scope — rather than an unfalsifiable "no model could be
    93	built." Building the service on a foundation that required concealed
    94	weakening would be theater; that, and only that, is what the original
    95	sentence forbids.
    96	
    97	---
    98	
    99	## A1.2 The pre-registered properties (the theorem statements)
   100	
   101	These are the properties the Band 0 work must establish, stated in prose
   102	*before* any modeling begins, so that "the proof holds" has a fixed
   103	referent. Each names how it is discharged: **[model]** — proven in the
   104	machine-checked model under idealized primitives (Layer 1); **[proof]** —
   105	proven directly (not a state-machine claim); **[assumption]** — cited as an
   106	explicit Layer 2 assumption, not proven. Restating a property here in
   107	sharper form does not weaken the original's hard invariants; where an
   108	invariant (§4.3, §4.6) and a property overlap, the property is the
   109	checkable form of the invariant.
   110	
   111	Temporal vocabulary used throughout (defined precisely in A1.6):
   112	`declared_issue_time` is what the issuer claims; `anchor_time` is the
   113	Bitcoin block time of the OTS anchor, which establishes only that the
   114	anchored bytes existed *not after* that block.
   115	
   116	- **P1 — Integrity (the headline claim).** There is no reachable state in
   117	  which the verifier returns `VALID_STRICT` or `VALID_DEGRADED` for a
   118	  package whose canonical bytes differ from bytes signed at issue time.
   119	  **[model]**
   120	
   121	- **P2 — Signature-set completeness (anti-stripping).** The canonical
   122	  signed bytes commit to the required-signer manifest. A package presenting
   123	  fewer signatures than its manifest requires yields `INVALID` — never
   124	  `VALID_STRICT`, and `VALID_DEGRADED` only under an explicit, recorded
   125	  policy within the waivable set of A1.2.1. Consequence: "issued with one
   126	  signature" and "issued with two, one stripped" are distinguishable by
   127	  construction, because the expected set is inside what every signature
   128	  signs. **[model]**
   129	
   130	- **P3 — Key binding (anti-substitution).** Acceptance binds each
   131	  signature to the issuer identity committed in the signed bytes. The
   132	  adversary model (A1.3) explicitly includes key-substitution attacks
   133	  (DSKS / exclusive-ownership failures: constructing a different keypair
   134	  under which an existing message–signature pair verifies, then
   135	  re-presenting the package under a substituted manifest). Idealized
   136	  signatures assume this binding by fiat; Ed25519 signature verification
   137	  alone does not provide protocol-level issuer binding or exclusive
   138	  ownership — the verification equation is not, by itself, the
   139	  issuer-binding protocol. Discharge is split:
   140	  - the binding of identity into signed bytes — the signed bytes include,
   141	    at minimum: domain-separation tag, object type (per P7's enumeration),
   142	    algorithm identifier, issuer identity, key fingerprint, manifest hash,
   143	    and canonicalization version — is **[model]**;
   144	  - the implementation's **verification profile** is **[assumption]**,
   145	    named in Layer 2 and discharged as H1a evidence: exact library and
   146	    version, accepted signature encodings, public-key validation behavior,
   147	    low-order-point handling, cofactor equation variant, and negative test
   148	    vectors for each, all recorded in the repository.
   149	
   150	  This property may not fall between the layers.
   151	
   152	- **P4 — Fail-closed state logic.** The verifier's four states partition
   153	  all outcomes. A required check that fails yields `INVALID`. A check that
   154	  cannot be performed yields `UNVERIFIABLE` — never any `VALID` state,
   155	  under any trace. `VALID_DEGRADED` arises only from an explicit, recorded
   156	  policy within A1.2.1's waivable set, never as a default or a fallback.
   157	  (The checkable form of §4.6.) **[model]**
   158	
   159	- **P5 — Temporal soundness (two-sided).** The anchor is an upper bound on
   160	  creation time, and only that (A1.6). Strict verification requires the
   161	  anchor to be consistent with the declared issue time **in both
   162	  directions**:
   163	
   164	      declared_issue_time − ε  ≤  anchor_time  ≤  declared_issue_time + δ
   165	
   166	  The δ side **bounds** *backdating*: a forger signing new bytes cannot
   167	  obtain a conforming anchor for a claimed time more than δ in the past —
   168	  backdating within the tolerance window is not eliminated, only bounded.
   169	  The ε side **bounds** *post-dating*: an issuer cannot declare an issue
   170	  time more than ε after the anchor and later present the receipt as
   171	  fresh.
   172	
   173	  **δ and ε belong to the verifier, not the receipt.** A receipt records
   174	  its issue-time policy version and the observed anchor delay; it may not
   175	  choose its own temporal tolerances — a receipt-controlled tolerance
   176	  would let a malicious issuer write an enormous window into the signed
   177	  bytes. The reference verifier's strict policy fixes the maxima: **δ =
   178	  72 hours** (OTS aggregation-to-confirmation lag plus Bitcoin timestamp
   179	  skew), **ε = 24 hours** (Bitcoin block timestamps may lag real time by
   180	  hours under the median-time-past rule). A verifier may choose stricter
   181	  bounds; no degraded policy may enlarge δ or ε beyond the strict maxima.
   182	  Both maxima are ratified (or revised, on the record) at Band 0 exit.
   183	  Temporal-anchor consistency is **non-waivable** (A1.2.1). **[model]**,
   184	  resting on the Bitcoin/OTS assumptions of A1.6 **[assumption]**.
   185	
   186	  *Issuance-protocol corollary (so honest receipts cannot rot):* issuance
   187	  is not complete until the anchor is **confirmed** — buried at a minimum
   188	  confirmation depth **k** on the Bitcoin chain (strict default **k = 6**,
   189	  ratified with δ and ε at Band 0 exit) — within δ of
   190	  `declared_issue_time`. Depth is what "confirmed" *means*: a
   191	  one-confirmation anchor orphaned by a transient reorganization would
   192	  leave an already-shipped receipt permanently unverifiable, so a receipt
   193	  never ships on a shallow anchor. If confirmation is delayed past δ for
   194	  benign reasons (calendar outage, fee spikes, reorg), the receipt is
   195	  **re-issued** with a fresh declared time and re-anchored — the failed
   196	  attempt is discarded, not shipped. Without this rule, honest-but-late
   197	  anchoring would be permanently indistinguishable from backdating.
   198	
   199	- **P6 — Revocation time-relativity (evaluated over the uncertainty
   200	  window).** The anchor proves only that the signed bytes existed *not
   201	  after* `anchor_time`; it cannot locate the signing moment inside
   202	  [`declared_issue_time`, `anchor_time`]. Evaluating authorization only
   203	  at `declared_issue_time` would therefore admit a revocation/backdating
   204	  interaction: a key revoked inside the δ window could sign *after*
   205	  revocation while declaring a time just before it, and pass both P5 and
   206	  a point-evaluated P6. Accordingly, `VALID_STRICT` requires
   207	  authorization **throughout the uncertainty window**:
   208	
   209	      key_authorized(declared_issue_time)
   210	      ∧ no revocation effective at or before anchor_time
   211	
   212	  Revocation effective *after* `anchor_time` does not retroactively
   213	  change the verdict. Revocation effective at or before `anchor_time`
   214	  yields `INVALID`.
   215	
   216	  **Key lifecycle is monotonic, by design commitment.** Revocation is
   217	  terminal: a revoked key is never re-authorized; re-keying issues a *new*
   218	  key with a new fingerprint and a new manifest entry. Without
   219	  monotonicity, "no revocation effective at or before `anchor_time`"
   220	  would be ambiguous across revoke–re-authorize cycles; with it, the
   221	  interval check is well-defined. This is an issuer key-management rule
   222	  that the model *assumes* and operational policy *enforces* — a design
   223	  commitment, not a cryptographic assumption, and deliberately not filed
   224	  under Layer 2.
   225	
   226	  This interval evaluation is deliberately stricter than checking
   227	  `declared_issue_time` alone, and it can fail honest receipts whose key
   228	  was revoked between declaration and anchor confirmation — the correct
   229	  fail-closed outcome: the system cannot honestly prove such a receipt
   230	  was signed before the revocation, and the issuance protocol (P5
   231	  corollary) already treats unconfirmed issuance as incomplete. (The
   232	  checkable form of §4.4's revocation paragraph, in A1.6's vocabulary.)
   233	  **[model]**
   234	
   235	- **P7 — Wrapper and object-type soundness.** Every signed object carries
   236	  a domain-separation type tag *inside the signed bytes*, drawn from the
   237	  enumerated set: base attestation, wrapper, issuer-key manifest,
   238	  authority evidence, conformance vector, review-recency attestation
   239	  (§4.7). No trace exists in which an object of one type is accepted as
   240	  another (no cross-type confusion). A wrapper commits to the inner
   241	  package's **exact bytes, embedded as an opaque byte string** (e.g.,
   242	  base64 of the inner package's framed bytes) — never as parsed JSON.
   243	  Outer canonicalization must be structurally unable to re-serialize,
   244	  re-escape, or otherwise touch the inner byte stream: a nested-JSON
   245	  embedding would let a different outer canonicalization version silently
   246	  alter the inner bytes and break the inner signatures and OTS proof.
   247	  (Hash-only commitment is rejected: the inner bytes must travel *inside*
   248	  the bundle, or P9's self-containment fails.) The wrapper records both
   249	  inner and outer canonicalization versions. Wrapping never alters the
   250	  inner package's
   251	  independently-computed verdict — the wrapper attests the inner *bytes*,
   252	  never the inner *verification result* (preserving §3.1's distinction
   253	  exactly). **[model]**
   254	
   255	- **P8 — Canonicalization injectivity, framing, and rejection.**
   256	  `canonical()` is injective on its **accepted payload domain**, and the
   257	  encoding is boundary-unambiguous. JCS (RFC 8785) itself carries no
   258	  length header, so the frame supplies it: signatures and anchors commit
   259	  to a **framed envelope** —
   260	  `type_tag || canonicalization_version || payload_length || payload` —
   261	  where `payload` is the JCS bytes. The *issuer* constructs the frame; the
   262	  *verifier* reconstructs it independently and rejects any mismatch; the
   263	  exact binary layout is fixed, with golden vectors, before the Band 1
   264	  freeze. A parser cannot disagree about where the payload ends. The
   265	  accepted domain is
   266	  closed by explicit *rejection*: anything outside it is refused, never
   267	  "helpfully" normalized. Rejection is required, at minimum, for:
   268	  duplicate object names; `NaN`/`Infinity`; numbers not exactly
   269	  representable as IEEE 754 doubles (per §4.3, such values must be
   270	  string-encoded); `-0` and numeric edge cases; non-I-JSON strings;
   271	  inputs relying on Unicode normalization assumptions; unknown
   272	  canonicalization versions; trailing bytes; type-tag mismatch;
   273	  length-prefix mismatch. Golden vectors (§4.3) evidence the positive
   274	  domain; the rejection suite evidences the negative domain — both are
   275	  H1a obligations. Injectivity over the accepted domain is discharged by
   276	  direct proof. **[proof]**
   277	
   278	- **P9 — Verification statelessness.** The verdict is a pure function of
   279	  the bundle and the verifier's declared policy — no service-side state,
   280	  no live network dependency, appears in the decision. (The checkable form
   281	  of §4.4's hard rule.) **[model]**
   282	
   283	- **P10 — Manifest authority (external evidence plus possession).** The
   284	  issuer-key manifest carries **two independent, archived, time-anchored
   285	  authority-publication evidences** — the DNSSEC snapshot and the anchored
   286	  repository publication (A1.5) — **plus** a manifest self-signature by the
   287	  issuer keys. The self-signature proves *possession* and binds the
   288	  manifest bytes to the asserted keys; it is **not authority evidence and
   289	  is never counted as an authority channel** — an adversary substituting
   290	  their own key self-signs trivially. `VALID_STRICT` requires **all**
   291	  external authority evidences to validate, plus proof-of-possession.
   292	  Property: an adversary controlling any proper subset of the external
   293	  authority channels cannot cause a manifest asserting a non-authorized
   294	  key to satisfy the `VALID_STRICT` evidence requirement. Missing or
   295	  partially-validating evidence degrades by A1.2.1's explicit-policy
   296	  logic, naming which authority evidence was waived and why — never
   297	  silently. **[model]**
   298	
   299	**Change discipline.** This list may be amended before Band 0 exit only by
   300	a further signed, dated amendment stating what changed and why — the same
   301	rule as everything else in the pre-registration. Silent narrowing of the
   302	property list is the failure mode this section exists to prevent.
   303	
   304	### A1.2.1 Degraded-policy semantics (the waiver lattice)
   305	
   306	`VALID_DEGRADED` exists so long-horizon verification does not collapse to
   307	a boolean — not as a universal escape hatch. Its scope is fixed here:
   308	
   309	**Non-waivable checks.** No verifier policy may waive: canonical-byte
   310	integrity (P1, P8); type/domain separation (P7); boundary framing (P8);
   311	key-to-issuer binding for every accepted signature (P3); temporal-anchor
   312	consistency (P5). A package failing any non-waivable check is `INVALID`
   313	under **every** policy — no degraded policy may promote it
   314	(*monotonicity*). A non-waivable check that *cannot be performed* yields
   315	`UNVERIFIABLE`, never any `VALID` state — the P4 partition applies inside
   316	the lattice: performed-and-failed → `INVALID`; unperformable →
   317	`UNVERIFIABLE`; neither is promotable.
   318	
   319	**Waivable checks (declared redundancy only).** Degraded policies may
   320	weaken only declared redundancy requirements: accepting a subset of the
   321	issue-time signature set (P2); accepting fewer than all external
   322	manifest-authority evidences (P10); accepting a signature whose trust root
   323	is no longer independently recoverable, where the remaining checks pass.
   324	
   325	**Recording.** Every `VALID_DEGRADED` verdict records the precise waived
   326	check set and the policy that authorized the waiver — the informed-consent
   327	act of §3.1, made auditable.
   328	
   329	---
   330	
   331	## A1.3 The adversary model (explicit capabilities)
   332	
   333	The Band 0 adversary can, at minimum:
   334	
   335	1. **Alter** any bytes of a package, receipt, manifest, or wrapper after
   336	   issue.
   337	2. **Strip, reorder, or duplicate** signatures within the signature set.
   338	3. **Substitute keys** — including choosing keypairs *after* seeing valid
   339	   signatures (the DSKS capability of P3) — and always self-sign with keys
   340	   it holds (possession is free to the adversary; see P10).
   341	4. **Replay** valid packages, receipts, or manifests in other contexts, and
   342	   re-frame objects across the P7 type boundaries.
   343	5. **Craft manifests** freely, and anchor anything: anchoring proves
   344	   existence at a time, not authority (A1.5, A1.6).
   345	6. **Control any proper subset of the external manifest-authority
   346	   channels** (P10).
   347	7. **Drive the legitimate issuance path** with authorized credentials —
   348	   named to re-affirm the original's misissuance boundary: this adversary
   349	   is *out of scope for the cryptographic construction* and is met by
   350	   operational controls (§3.1, threat classes). The model documents the
   351	   boundary; it does not pretend to cover it.
   352	
   353	The adversary cannot break the idealized primitives — signature
   354	unforgeability, hash collision/preimage resistance, or the temporal-anchor
   355	assumptions — which are the cited Layer 2 assumptions (original H0; A1.6).
   356	
   357	---
   358	
   359	## A1.4 Tooling: the right tool per target, not the familiar tool
   360	
   361	The original said "TLA+ and/or Lean4." That was reached for on familiarity,
   362	and the review contested it — recorded here in the §6
   363	disagreements-on-the-record spirit:
   364	
   365	- **The verifier state machine and temporal logic (P1, P2, P4, P5, P6, P7,
   366	  P9, P10)** — TLA+. This *is* state-machine work, TLA+'s home turf, and
   367	  the author can read and defend TLA+ cold, which the agreement gate
   368	  requires.
   369	- **The composition under an active adversary (P1–P3, P7 under A1.3)** —
   370	  a symbolic protocol verifier, **Tamarin or ProVerif**, whose built-in
   371	  Dolev-Yao adversary derives, replays, and substitutes messages natively.
   372	  Hand-rolling an adversary inside TLA+ would make the proof's value rest
   373	  on the fidelity of that hand-rolled adversary — reintroducing the
   374	  wrong-model risk at the exact spot it is hardest to review.
   375	- **Canonicalization injectivity (P8)** — direct proof over the encoding
   376	  rules (Lean4 if warranted, rigorous prose proof plus the golden-vector
   377	  and rejection suites if that is honest sufficiency), separate from the
   378	  state-machine work.
   379	
   380	**Tool-choice discipline (anti-tool-shopping).** "Tamarin or ProVerif"
   381	must not become "whichever tool proves the desired result." The choice is
   382	fixed *before* theorem-proving begins: a bounded spike may compare the two
   383	only on **expressiveness and author-readability** for P1–P3/P7 under A1.3
   384	— not on which yields the proof. The choice, the rejected alternative, and
   385	the reason are committed before substantive proof work starts. Switching
   386	tools later requires a signed amendment naming the blocker.
   387	
   388	**The readability trade-off, named.** The author reads TLA+ but not (yet)
   389	Tamarin's lemma language or ProVerif's applied pi calculus. A tool the
   390	author cannot audit weakens the human half of the agreement gate exactly
   391	where it matters most. Mitigation, committed to here: every
   392	symbolic-verifier lemma carries a **prose mapping** back to the A1.2
   393	property it discharges, and the informal written proof (H0 requirement 3)
   394	covers the composition argument in the author's own words regardless of
   395	tool.
   396	
   397	**Cross-model correspondence (the two-formalisms risk, named).** The
   398	construction is formalized twice — a TLA+ state machine and a symbolic
   399	protocol model — and two formalisms of "the same" system can quietly
   400	describe two subtly different systems, each verified, neither wrong,
   401	jointly meaningless. The informal written proof must therefore include an
   402	explicit **correspondence mapping** between the TLA+ transitions and the
   403	symbolic tool's rules/facts, arguing that both describe the same
   404	construction; the model-derived conformance vectors (A1.7) test both
   405	against the same implementation, which bounds — but does not eliminate —
   406	the divergence risk.
   407	
   408	Learning to read the symbolic tool's output well enough to contest
   409	it is part of the capability H0 demonstrates — the deskilling defense
   410	(§6), applied at the new edge.
   411	
   412	---
   413	
   414	## A1.5 Manifest authority: resolving the circularity
   415	
   416	The review surfaced a hole in §4.4 as written: the bundle's issuer-key
   417	manifest was to carry "timestamped evidence that those keys were authorized
   418	issuer keys at issue time, the manifest itself anchored." But **anchoring
   419	proves existence, not authority** — anyone can anchor a manifest claiming
   420	anything. A manifest signed by the keys it authorizes is circular; a
   421	manifest signed by a separate root key reintroduces the single trust
   422	domain §3.1 rejects.
   423	
   424	**Resolution (not deferral): apply §3.1's own argument recursively.**
   425	Authority at issue time cannot be conjured from nothing — every PKI bottoms
   426	out in trust anchors; the honest move is to make the anchors plural,
   427	independent, archived, and time-anchored. The manifest carries **two
   428	external authority-publication evidences plus proof-of-possession**:
   429	
   430	1. **DNSSEC chain snapshot** — the `wamason.com` records publishing the
   431	   issuer-key fingerprints, with the signature chain to the root as it
   432	   existed at issue time, *archived in the bundle* (discovery channels stay
   433	   discovery; the archived snapshot is what verification uses).
   434	2. **Anchored repository publication** — the public git commit publishing
   435	   the same fingerprints, with the commit object, its signature, the
   436	   relevant proof material, and the OTS proof **archived in the bundle**
   437	   (like the DNSSEC snapshot: verification uses the archive, never live
   438	   repository availability — P9).
   439	3. **Manifest self-signature** by the issuer keys — *proof of possession
   440	   only*. It binds the manifest bytes to the asserted keys and is never
   441	   counted as authority evidence (P10): an attacker's invented key
   442	   self-signs for free.
   443	
   444	`VALID_STRICT` requires both external evidences plus possession. A future
   445	verifier sees that at issue time, two channels with independent operational
   446	failure modes asserted the same key authority, and the asserted keys were
   447	demonstrably held. Subverting the process requires tampering with both
   448	external channels, at or before issue time, consistently.
   449	
   450	**Declared residual risks (difficulty, not impossibility):**
   451	
   452	- **Correlated control.** Both external channels are ultimately operated by
   453	  the same author — same registrar sphere, same person. Their independence
   454	  is real against *channel failure* (registrar lapse vs. repository-host
   455	  lapse) and against an attacker who compromises one channel; it is weaker
   456	  against an adversary who owns the author's entire operational sphere at
   457	  issue time. That adversary sits at the misissuance boundary (A1.3, item
   458	  7) and is met operationally, not cryptographically.
   459	- **Trust-anchor recursion.** The archived DNSSEC chain validates against
   460	  the root trust anchors *as they existed at issue time*. A verifier
   461	  decades later needs trustworthy knowledge of those historical anchors —
   462	  which is itself a historical-authority question. Concretely, the
   463	  reference verifier maintains an **archived historical trust-anchor
   464	  store** (DNS root keys and repository signing keys, by validity period),
   465	  distributed *with the verifier* as trust configuration — not
   466	  service-side state, so P9 is not violated. The plural-evidence
   467	  structure and the §3.1 wrapping model (re-anchoring under fresh trust
   468	  *before* old roots decay) mitigate the remaining recursion; they do not
   469	  eliminate it.
   470	
   471	Not *perfect* — plural evidence is a difficulty argument, not an
   472	impossibility proof, and it is declared as such — but *difficult*, and P10
   473	makes the difficulty claim checkable.
   474	
   475	---
   476	
   477	## A1.6 Temporal anchor semantics, stated precisely
   478	
   479	The original's forgery argument ("producing a valid OTS anchor at the
   480	original time") compressed what the anchor actually establishes. Precisely:
   481	
   482	- **Vocabulary.** `declared_issue_time` — the issue time the issuer claims,
   483	  committed inside the signed canonical bytes. `anchor_time` — the Bitcoin
   484	  block time of the OTS anchor over those bytes.
   485	- **The anchor is an upper bound on creation time.** The anchor proves the
   486	  bytes existed *not after* `anchor_time` — nothing more. It provides **no
   487	  lower bound**: old bytes can be stamped today, and a forger with
   488	  compromised keys can sign new bytes today. What the anchor prevents is
   489	  *backdating* — obtaining a conforming anchor in the past for bytes
   490	  created now. New forgeries dated honestly-now are the key-compromise /
   491	  misissuance classes (§3.1), met operationally, not by the anchor.
   492	- **The verifier enforces a two-sided consistency check (P5).**
   493	  `declared_issue_time − ε ≤ anchor_time ≤ declared_issue_time + δ`, with
   494	  strict defaults δ = 72 hours and ε = 24 hours, both declared per receipt
   495	  and ratified (or revised, on the record) at Band 0 exit. Temporal
   496	  consistency is non-waivable (A1.2.1). The issuance protocol treats
   497	  anchor confirmation — at minimum depth k, strict default k = 6 (P5
   498	  corollary) — within δ as part of successful issuance; late or reorged
   499	  anchors force re-issue.
   500	- **Layer 2 assumptions, extended.** To the original's cited assumptions
   501	  (signature unforgeability, SHA-256 resistance, OTS/Bitcoin timestamp
   502	  assumptions) add, explicitly: Bitcoin block timestamps are inexact —
   503	  consensus rules (median-time-past, the two-hour future bound) bound but
   504	  do not eliminate skew — absorbed into δ and ε; anchors at depth ≥ k are
   505	  treated as permanent (a reorganization deeper than k is out of model,
   506	  subsumed by the history assumption that follows); and **the
   507	  proof-of-work history remains intact and available over the
   508	  adjudication horizon**. A
   509	  fifty-year adjudication story rests on Bitcoin's fifty-year survival;
   510	  stating that plainly is the declare-what-you-cannot-see ethos, applied
   511	  to the anchor itself.
   512	
   513	---
   514	
   515	## A1.7 The agreement gate, hardened
   516	
   517	The original gate asked author and AI to independently *agree* the model is
   518	faithful. Confirmation tasks produce weak evidence: a reviewer re-reads the
   519	author's framing and nods, because the framing is persuasive — that is why
   520	the author chose it. And the AI collaborator that helped author the model
   521	agreeing with it is close to self-review. Two changes, both consistent with
   522	§4.5's different-model discipline, applied one level up:
   523	
   524	1. **The fidelity review is a falsification task performed by non-author
   525	   models.** Models that had no hand in authoring the formal model receive
   526	   the A1.2 property list (prose) and the model itself, and are tasked
   527	   adversarially: *construct a concrete trace the model accepts that the
   528	   properties say must be rejected, or a property the prose implies that
   529	   the model fails to state.* A reviewer who hunted for a counterexample
   530	   and failed is evidence; a reviewer who agreed is a nod. The hunt's
   531	   artifacts are committed either way — found or not found — and each
   532	   review records, for reproducibility: model, vendor, and date; the
   533	   prompts used; configuration where available; the hash of the exact
   534	   input bundle; and whether the reviewer saw prior review transcripts.
   535	
   536	2. **Model-derived conformance vectors.** The model checker's explored
   537	   traces are mechanically extracted into concrete fixtures for the real
   538	   verifier — each trace names inputs and the verdict the model requires
   539	   (e.g., "manifest requires {KMS, GPG}, bundle carries only KMS →
   540	   `INVALID`"). This narrows the declared model-to-code gap (the code is
   541	   tested against the model's exact predictions, not hand-written
   542	   approximations of them) and doubles as a fidelity check: an unfaithful
   543	   model, concretized into fixtures, produces *visibly absurd test cases*
   544	   — far easier to catch than a subtle misstatement in temporal logic.
   545	   These vectors become part of the H1a suite, with a clean division of
   546	   labor: model-derived vectors *evidence* conformance to the model on the
   547	   extracted traces — testing, not proof of implementation/model
   548	   equivalence, preserving the original's attested-not-proven discipline
   549	   for the model-to-code gap; the adversarially-authored red-bar attacks
   550	   (§4.5) hunt for what is *outside* the model. **Declared limitation:** the trace-to-fixture translation
   551	   is itself a step with fidelity risk — abstract states must be
   552	   concretized into real byte-level bundles. The concretizer is therefore
   553	   reviewed under the same non-author discipline, and its output is
   554	   spot-audited by eye; the risk is named, not waved off.
   555	
   556	**Declared residual risk — self-adjudication.** Whether a reviewer's
   557	counterexample stands or is refuted is judged by the author. Independent
   558	adjudication costs money the pre-registered no-spend constraint (H3)
   559	forbids; the softness is therefore declared rather than concealed, and
   560	every adjudication (counterexample accepted or refuted, with reasoning) is
   561	committed alongside the review artifacts, so a future reader can re-judge.
   562	
